.Dd October 13, 2026
.Dt CBLINK-GEN 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm cblink-gen
.Nd generate C code and documentation from a cblink family spec
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl BnV
.Op Fl b Ar base
.Op Fl m Ar parts
.Op Fl o Ar dir
.Ar spec.yaml
.Nm
.Fl n | d
.Op Fl a
.Op Fl F Ar module : Ns Ar feature , Ns ...
.Op Fl p Ar dir
.Op Fl s Ar file.sid
.Ar module.yang ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility reads the description of a
.Xr cblink 3
family from
.Ar spec.yaml
and writes the C code that servers and clients of that family use:
typed request and reply structures, policies, parsers, builders,
client wrappers and server registration.
It plays the part YNL plays for Linux netlink.
The spec format, a subset of YAML, is described in
.Pa docs/SPEC-FORMAT.md
in the cblink sources.
.Pp
The output is deterministic: the same spec always gives byte-identical
files, so generated code can be committed and checked.
Files are written atomically: each is created under a temporary name in
.Ar dir
and renamed into place, so an interrupted run leaves the previous files
intact.
.Pp
The spec is untrusted input.
.Nm
opens the spec and the output directory, then enters
.Xr capsicum 4
capability mode before it parses anything.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width "-m parts"
.It Fl a
For YANG modules: give a SID to each item of the module of every
.Fl s
file that has none, and rewrite the file.
The items are numbered as RFC 9595 Appendix B says: by namespace
(module, identities, features, data), each in identifier order, every
one the next SID free in the file's assignment ranges.
A file that had items gets the next
.Ql sid-file-version ,
and its
.Ql dependency-revision
becomes the revisions the module's imports are bound to.
Running it again on a complete file changes nothing.
.It Fl B
Allow what is reserved for families built into the library:
the family names
.Ql ctrl
and
.Ql cblink.* ,
and a
.Ql fixed-id .
Only the control family uses this.
.It Fl d
For YANG modules: print the generator's model of the compiled schema,
a schema node a line (its module, kind, path and flags), for comparison
with
.Xr yanglint 1 .
.It Fl F Ar module : Ns Ar feature , Ns ...
For YANG modules: the features of
.Ar module
to enable,
.Ql *
for all of them, none for none.
It may be given up to 64 times.
Without
.Fl F ,
every feature of every implemented module is on; with it, only those
named are, as with
.Xr yanglint 1 .
What a disabled feature guards is left out, identities, enums and bits
included; the deviations of the modules loaded are applied.
.It Fl b Ar base
Name the output files
.Ar base Ns .h ,
.Ar base Ns .c
and so on.
The default is the spec's file name without
.Pa .yaml .
.It Fl m Ar parts
A comma-separated list of the files to write:
.Bl -tag -width "server"
.It Cm h
.Ar base Ns .h :
constants, attribute and command enumerations, structures and all
prototypes.
.It Cm c
.Ar base Ns .c :
policies, parsers and builders, needed by both sides.
.It Cm client
.Ar base Ns _client.c :
client wrappers for requests (synchronous, and asynchronous for loop
callbacks), dumps, subscriptions and streams.
.It Cm server
.Ar base Ns _server.c :
the family definition, registration and reply helpers; the server
supplies the handler functions declared in the header.
.It Cm md
.Ar base Ns .md :
reference documentation in Markdown.
.El
.Pp
The default is
.Ql h,c,client,server,md .
.It Fl n
Check the spec and report problems without writing anything.
For a YANG module, report what was compiled.
.It Fl p Ar dir
Look for the modules a YANG module imports in
.Ar dir ,
as well as in the module's own directory.
It may be given up to 16 times.
.It Fl s Ar file.sid
For YANG modules: attach the YANG Schema Item iDentifiers of a module
from an RFC 9595
.Pa .sid
file, and verify them: no SID given twice, none outside the file's
assignment ranges, and every item of the module with one, empty
.Ql input
and
.Ql output
included.
It may be given up to 16 times.
.It Fl o Ar dir
Write the files into
.Ar dir
instead of the current directory.
.It Fl V
Print the version and exit.
.El
.Ss YANG modules
A file named
.Ar name Ns .yang
or
.Ar name Ns @ Ns Ar revision Ns .yang
is a YANG module, read through libyang when
.Nm
is built with it
.Pq Va CBLINK_GEN_LIBYANG ,
which it is when libyang 3 is installed.
Every module it may import is read before capability mode is entered,
from its own directory and each
.Fl p
directory; a module imported without a revision is the latest found.
Several may be named.
Only
.Fl n
and
.Fl d
are supported yet: the modules are parsed and compiled, and errors are
reported as for a spec.
A
.Nm
built without libyang refuses YANG modules.
.Ss Building with make
.Pa ${LOCALBASE}/share/cblink/cblink.mk
runs
.Nm
from a
.Xr bsd.prog.mk 5
Makefile:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
PROG=		kvd
SRCS=		kvd.c
CBLINK_SPECS=	kv.yaml
\&.include "${LOCALBASE}/share/cblink/cblink.mk"
\&.include <bsd.prog.mk>
.Ed
.Pp
See the comment at the top of
.Pa cblink.mk
for its variables.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "${LOCALBASE}/share/cblink/specs/control.yaml" -compact
.It Pa ${LOCALBASE}/share/cblink/cblink.mk
make rules for programs built from specs
.It Pa ${LOCALBASE}/share/cblink/specs/control.yaml
the control family's spec, an example of the format
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width "0"
.It 0
Success.
.It 1
The spec is invalid; every problem found is reported on standard error as
.Ar file : Ns Ar line : Ns Ar column : Li error: Ar message .
.It 2
Usage error, or an output or system error.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Check a spec, then generate everything but the documentation into
.Pa obj :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ cblink-gen -n kv.yaml
$ cblink-gen -m h,c,client,server -o obj kv.yaml
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr cblink 3 ,
.Xr capsicum 4
